#{[17592186357723 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357313 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357723 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94311" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357723 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357723 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+2y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing heavy damping\n\nII. ${y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+7y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing light damping\n\nIII. ${y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+16y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing critical  damping" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357723 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c6276abb-11ab-5f2c-8db5-0bce52daa784" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357723 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that, given the equation of a damped harmonic oscillator, the type of damping depends on the discriminant of the characteristic equation:\n\n- $D>0$ leads to heavy damping,\n\n- $D=0$ leads to critical damping, and\n\n- $D<0$ leads to light damping.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+2y$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +2=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={6}^{2}-4(1)(2) & =28>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents heavy damping, and so statement I is true.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+7y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+5\\lambda +7=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={5}^{2}-4(1)(7) & =-3<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents light damping, and so statement II is true.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+16y$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +16=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={8}^{2}-4(1)(16) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents critical damping, and so statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357725 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357171 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357725 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94313" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357725 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357725 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{″}+2\\sqrt{2}{y}^{′}+2y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing heavy damping\n\nII. ${y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+9y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing critical damping\n\nIII. ${y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+14y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing light damping" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357725 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2840cb37-803e-5355-a4ea-a188990899c3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357725 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that, given the equation of a damped harmonic oscillator, the type of damping depends on the discriminant of the characteristic equation:\n\n- $D>0$ leads to heavy damping,\n\n- $D=0$ leads to critical damping, and\n\n- $D<0$ leads to light damping.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+2\\sqrt{2}{y}^{′}+2y$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\sqrt{2}\\lambda +2=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D=(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}-4(1)(2) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents critical damping, and so statement I is false.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+9y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+7\\lambda +9=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={7}^{2}-4(1)(9) & =13>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents heavy damping, and so statement II is false.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+14y$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+7\\lambda +14=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={7}^{2}-4(1)(14) & =-7<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents light damping, and so statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357727 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357043 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357727 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94321" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357727 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357727 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle of mass $4\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=10\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$  The particle is also subject to a resistive force that's proportional to the particle's velocity, and has a damping coefficient $c=8\\,\\text{kg/s}.$  The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  If the particle moves with damped harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357727 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a9da3526-636b-5179-9f25-351d09bfb11d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357727 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation of damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-c{y}^{′}-ky.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k$ and $c$ gives\n\n$$\n4{y}^{″}=-8{y}^{′}-10y.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+\\frac{5}{2}y=0.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392870 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186392871 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392870 #edb/ref 1069 "q-64623" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392870 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392870 #edb/ref 1071 "The position $y(t)$ of a particle oscillating with light damped harmonic motion is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+52y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. What is the period of the damped oscillation?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392870 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9e801805-48d7-5f7f-a2eb-42e2eeee70b1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392870 #edb/ref 1074 "The given equation represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. We are looking for solutions of the form $y={e}^{\\lambda t}.$\n\nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +52=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +52 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +16)-16+52 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)^{2}+36 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)^{2} & =-36 \\\\ \\lambda +4 & =\\pm 6\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-4\\pm 6\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}={e}^{-4t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡6t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡6t).\n$$\n\nFrom the solution, the value of the angular frequency is $Ω=6\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}.$\n\nThe period of the damped oscillation $T$ is related to the angular frequency as follows:\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{Ω}\n$$\n\nSo the period is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{6}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\,\\text{s}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392871 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186392872 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392871 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186392873 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392871 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186392874 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392871 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186392875 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392871 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186392876 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392871 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392871 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392871 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186392872 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392871 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f29649ee-a16d-56d9-80ba-8cd64f7d549a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392872 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\,\\text{s}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392872 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392872 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9d7c71da-e88e-5ef7-9992-57e3285741a8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392873 #edb/ref 1080 "3\\pi \\,\\text{s}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392873 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392873 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "87ab7734-6709-5366-9804-65686fe38f5e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392874 #edb/ref 1080 "6\\pi \\,\\text{s}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392874 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392874 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "bc219892-8db2-59ec-8f0e-74228c0dcdd5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392875 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{2\\pi }{5}\\,\\text{s}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392875 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392875 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "90aaa559-d83e-58cb-8b19-dca2ca99dcd5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392876 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\,\\text{s}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392876 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392876 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "af845d3e-6ea7-51b2-9edf-f53410cbddd8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393940 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186393941 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393940 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79571" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393940 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393940 #edb/ref 1071 "The position $y(t)$ of a particle oscillating with light damped harmonic motion is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+41y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. What is the period of the damped oscillation?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393940 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5708124f-9743-5ab7-8b57-9c32436ebfac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393940 #edb/ref 1074 "The given equation represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. We are looking for solutions of the form $y={e}^{\\lambda t}.$\n\nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +41=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +41 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +16)-16+41 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)^{2}+25 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)^{2} & =-25 \\\\ \\lambda +4 & =\\pm 5\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-4\\pm 5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-4t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t).\n$$\n\nFrom the solution, the value of the angular frequency is $Ω=5\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}.$\n\nThe period of the damped oscillation $T$ is related to the angular frequency as follows:\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{Ω}\n$$\n\nSo the period is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{5}\\,\\text{s}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393941 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186393942 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393941 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186393943 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393941 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186393944 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393941 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186393945 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393941 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186393946 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393941 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393941 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393941 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186393944 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393941 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "519ee466-f0d0-576d-b575-b46d45151fdf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393942 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\,\\text{s}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393942 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393942 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fa9cb0a8-557e-5b0b-9753-10f618e7048e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393943 #edb/ref 108