186432822 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186432801 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432822 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "4bf5f75c-ce4c-503a-9ca3-208934e1466a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432823 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186432800 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432823 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186432802 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432823 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186432804 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432823 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186432807 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432823 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186432810 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432823 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432823 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432823 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186432807 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432823 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "578e80cb-b054-52ce-97be-509dd668d752" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432824 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186432797 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432824 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186432798 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432824 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186432803 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432824 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186432806 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432824 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186432814 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432824 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432824 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432824 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186432814 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432824 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "8174c21b-eafc-56be-ad59-cefe6b8b533d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432825 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186432799 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432825 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186432817 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432825 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186432818 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432825 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186432819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432825 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186432821 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432825 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432825 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432825 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186432821 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432825 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "bc9a2aa2-4314-5e20-994f-9b028b117d14" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432826 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186432824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432826 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94360" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432826 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432826 #edb/ref 1071 "The position $y(t)$ of a particle oscillating with light damped harmonic motion is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+25y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. What is the period of the damped oscillation?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432826 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6c4b5efd-8648-5dd6-b5f3-7b3ca42bb25b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432826 #edb/ref 1074 "The given equation represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. We are looking for solutions of the form $y={e}^{\\lambda t}.$\n\nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +25=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +25 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +9)-9+25 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2}+16 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2} & =-16 \\\\ \\lambda +3 & =\\pm 4\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-3\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}={e}^{-3t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t).\n$$\n\nFrom the solution, the value of the angular frequency is $Ω=4\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}.$\n\nThe period of the damped oscillation $T$ is related to the angular frequency as follows:\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{Ω}\n$$\n\nSo the period is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{4}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,\\text{s}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432827 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186432825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432827 #edb/ref 1069 "q-64587" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432827 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432827 #edb/ref 1071 "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t.$ Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing damped harmonic motion?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432827 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "76c1e10b-ff92-55c3-9f48-cde089c3ec98" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432827 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation of damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+b{y}^{′}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nComparing our equation with the above, we can see that the only equation that represents damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+4y=0.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432828 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186432823 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432828 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94314" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432828 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432828 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+4y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing critical damping\n\nII. ${y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+3y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing heavy damping\n\nIII. ${y}^{″}+12{y}^{′}+36y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing light damping" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432828 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9d285631-ec24-50a4-ab3c-c0601632cbad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432828 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that, given the equation of a damped harmonic oscillator, the type of damping depends on the discriminant of the characteristic equation:\n\n- $D>0$ leads to heavy damping,\n\n- $D=0$ leads to critical damping, and\n\n- $D<0$ leads to light damping.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+4y$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +4=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={8}^{2}-4(1)(4) & =48>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents heavy damping, and so statement I is false.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+3y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +3=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={4}^{2}-4(1)(3) & =4>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents heavy damping, and so statement II is true.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+12{y}^{′}+36y$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+12\\lambda +36=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={12}^{2}-4(1)(36) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents critical damping, and so statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432829 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186432822 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432829 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94320" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432829 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432829 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle of mass $3\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=18\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$  The particle is also subject to a resistive force that's proportional to the particle's velocity, and has a damping coefficient $c=6\\,\\text{kg/s}.$  The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  If the particle moves with damped harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432829 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "db14497d-6b9b-508e-a43a-c6705f9b9e88" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432829 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation of damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-c{y}^{′}-ky.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k$ and $c$ gives\n\n$$\n3{y}^{″}=-6{y}^{′}-18y.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+6y=0.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
